---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PdfEditFlattenPdf from '@/components/tools/pdf/PdfEditFlattenPdf.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="flatten-pdf">
  <PdfEditFlattenPdf client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>The problem flattening solves</h2>
      <p>
        You fill in a PDF form and email it, and the recipient says the boxes are empty. Or you print it
        and the answers are not on the paper. Or you upload it to a portal and the answers are removed. All
        of these are the same bug, and you did not cause it.
      </p>
      <p>
        A PDF page has content (text, lines, images) and annotations, which are separate objects on top of
        the page: form field widgets, highlights, sticky notes, stamps, ink. Readers do not draw them
        consistently, printers do not print them consistently, and any process that renders the page again
        can drop them. Flattening moves all of them into the page content, so they always show.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What this tool does that most "flatteners" do not</h2>
      <p>
        Flattening form fields is easy, and all tools do it. Annotations are harder: a highlight, a comment,
        a stamp, an ink signature added in a PDF reader. Many tools delete them, and because they were often
        invisible in your reader, you notice only when the printed copy is blank.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each annotation has an <strong>appearance stream</strong>: a small form XObject with the drawing
        instructions the reader uses to show it. This tool takes that stream, calculates the transform that
        maps the bounding box of the appearance onto the rectangle of the annotation (the algorithm in
        ISO 32000-1 §12.5.5), and draws it into the content of the page. Only then does it remove the
        annotation. For this reason, comments survive here.
      </p>
      <p>
        Annotations with no stored appearance stay as they are and are not discarded, and the result panel
        tells you how many there are. Deleting something visible without notice is worse than not flattening
        it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>When to flatten</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="min-w-[620px]">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Situation</th>
              <th scope="col">Flatten?</th>
              <th scope="col">Why</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Returning a completed application form</td>
              <td>Yes</td>
              <td>The reader always sees what you typed</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Sending a marked-up draft to a printer</td>
              <td>Yes</td>
              <td>Comments print and do not disappear</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Archiving a signed contract</td>
              <td>Yes</td>
              <td>No dependency on annotation support in future readers</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Circulating a form for others to complete</td>
              <td>No</td>
              <td>Flattening removes the fields they need</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Collaborative review still in progress</td>
              <td>No</td>
              <td>Comments become page ink that cannot be deleted</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Hiding confidential text</td>
              <td>No</td>
              <td>Flattening is not redaction; see <a href="/redact-pdf">Redact PDF</a></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Flattening is not rasterising</h2>
      <p>
        Some tools "flatten" a PDF by rendering each page to an image. That makes the file uniform, but it
        also destroys the text layer, removes search and screen-reader access, and usually makes the file
        several times larger.
      </p>
      <p>
        This tool rasterises nothing. Text stays text, vectors stay vectors, and images keep their original
        compression. Only the annotation and field layer changes. If you want pages turned into images (for
        redaction, or to prevent text extraction), use <a href="/redact-pdf">Redact PDF</a> or
        <a href="/grayscale-pdf">PDF to Grayscale</a>; both state this clearly.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Keep the original</h2>
      <p>
        Flattening cannot be undone: when the value of a field becomes page ink, there is no field to restore
        and no value to read. The file you drop in does not change, because the flattened copy is a new
        download. Keep the original until the recipient accepts the flattened version.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
